description:
## 🐛 Problem

`pkg/anthro/usage.go:writeCache` uses `os.WriteFile` directly. If two writers race — e.g. the TUI's 2-min poller fires while `ccpulse status --tmux` runs from a tmux statusline — they can interleave and produce a half-written `usage.json`. The next reader sees JSON parse failure and treats the cache as missing.

## ✅ Fix

Standard pattern: write to a `.tmp` sibling and `os.Rename` into place. POSIX rename is atomic on the same filesystem, so concurrent readers see either the old file or the new file — never a partial one.

```go
func writeCache(path string, u Usage, when time.Time) error {
    // ... marshal as before ...
    tmp := path + ".tmp"
    if err := os.WriteFile(tmp, out, 0644); err != nil {
        return err
    }
    return os.Rename(tmp, path)
}
```

## 🧪 Test plan

- [ ] Add a concurrent-write test in `pkg/anthro/usage_test.go`: spawn N goroutines each calling `writeCache` with distinct timestamps, then assert `readCache` succeeds and returns one of the N timestamps (never an error).
- [ ] Existing tests still pass.

## 🔗 Context

- Flagged in the PR #11 review as the only \"important\" hazard. Filed separately because it's a small, contained fix that warrants its own commit.
- Considered SQLite-backed cache as an alternative; rejected because the JSON blob isn't queryable and mixing into `state.db` couples unrelated lifecycles (a `state.db` integrity rebuild would blow away the usage cache).
